the shadow former rail mr. Jonathan  Reynolds mr. Reynolds thank you for  joining us why did you resign well let  me say the start I like Jeremy  personally a great deal i enjoyed the  work I did with him but one of the  reasons I think Jeremy is quite popular  within the labor movement is these  authentic he's true to what he believes  in and so I think if you're a shadow  minister and you want to be authentic  and true to what you believe in and you  have some real doubts about the course  that we've taken and I thought  particularly with the sacking of Pat  McFadden late last night that was the  wrong decision to make for that for the  wrong reasons if on principle as I did I  didn't agree with that decision I think  the honorable thing to do is to resign  and I think you can do that in a way  which still supports the party but makes  clear your own views your own principles  because of that so that's why I've  decided to leave this morning so you  resign because Pat McFadden was sacked  yes I think the decisions that were  given and I think the objections to the  statement that part made were were not  things that I could agree with I think  Pat gave a statement which is a vast  majority of leg party members and  British people would agree with and I  wasn't happy to see him sacked and the  reasons then that came out the reasons  that were given to endorse that second  were not things that I couldn't agree  with in addition to that to be honest  when I when I looked at the direction we  were taking in the reshuffle I just  thought the best thing for me to do as a  Labour MP loyal to the Labour pie wants  to see a Labour government was to have  the freedom to speak out from the back  benches about the things I think labour  should be doing and you know I think  it's nothing dishonorable about that I  think that's what we need more people to  do if they think that's the right thing  to do equally people want to serve  that's the right thing to do for them as  well but it's each and every MPs  decision and I hope you'll respect the  decision that I've made with respect you  are not being loyal to the party though  are you being loyal to Pat McFadden no I  wouldn't say that I think what I want to  see is a Labour government I want to see  the Labour Party in a position to become  a Labour government in future and if  there are serious doubts I've got about  the direction we're going in on certain  areas i think it is my duty to make  clear my own views on that I've got my  own mandating my own constituency my own  people to represent within that too and  I think that's important you know that  the party structures they're not just  entirely about the leadership and the  positions taken there about individual  MPs and if those MPs don't feel  they can honorably be on the front bench  than the honorable thing to do is to  resign so what's fundamentally changed  since you thought it was a good idea to  accept the job back in September apart  from Pat McFadden being attacked I still  I'm still pleased i took that job back  in September I think what was clear then  was there was an attempt to make a  broad-based as wide as possible at a  shadow administration as could be and  when Jeremy sent an olive branch to lots  of us who didn't vote for him I think  the honorable thing to do then was to  accept that and become part of that  shadow government but clearly that's not  worked out to anyone's advantage or its  felt that way and I think looking at  what is now required that's clearly  going to be a more disciplined if you  like more coherent message on foreign  defense policies I don't object at all  to the leaders right to do that if the  position that will be taking is laid pie  is not one that I'm entirely comfortable  with then I don't think I could be part  of not giving him long though happier to  settle into the job X Factor contestants  get longer than that well I'm not sure  where that's the case anymore but look  it's all about the decisions that have  taken not the time skill that they are  taking on this clearly being a big  change in the labor of print bench  yesterday late last night when those  decisions came out and I don't think  being true to my own conscience I could  have said that I think those decisions  are the right ones to make and so I just  felt I couldn't continue and I didn't  discuss that with anyone else other than  with my wife this morning I sent a  letter of resignation I tried to make  clear be very dignified and reasonable  with jeremy's to the reasons I thought I  couldn't continue and indeed to praise  him on the areas where I think he said  and done the right thing but if I don't  feel it's the right thing for me to do I  need people to respect that decision  it's he the right man to lead the Labour  Party he's got a huge Monday to lead  late by ona do not like nine waited but  clearly we've had four candidates will  leader we've all voted for different  people so we're not going to pretend we  didn't vote for different people just a  relatively short period of time moving  on but I think what we need in the party  let's be clear I'm not entirely  convinced to be honest that any of the  leadership candidates could have  honestly said they've been in ass would  be in a strong position to make the leg  part win the next general election had  any of them won I think that means that  we need a serious and quite difficult  debate about where labor is going and  there  limitations reasonable limitations that  are put on people who run the front  bench and if I don't want to accept  those limitations i want the chance to  speak about the economy about welfare  about foreign policy as well as  transport which is what I was a shadow  minister before if I want that freedom I  think I've got to do the honorable thing  and do it from the back benches oh that  was a politician sense and that's not  what I asked you I asked you whether mr.  Corbin was the right man to lead the  Labour Party but he hasn't mandates lead  load party and I said and I said at the  time that it's not that that's not what  I'm asking you so if I asked you a third  time would you say the same thing again  well he's the right man because he's the  man who won the election I think that's  what are you thinking the right man I'm  not much I didn't vote for Jeremy I've  never I've never denied that I different  foot forgery it's a public what do you  see the right man to leave the Labour  Party well I wouldn't have voted for him  and I didn't vote for him in the contest  but he is the right man because he has  the mandate to lead the Labour Party and  people like myself who are resigning  today we're not objecting I think to  that mandate we respect that Monday but  that doesn't mean we feel the right  thing for ourselves to do is to serve on  its front bench what about the other  front benchers what would you perhaps  say to them I'm thinking particularly if  people like I don't know Andy Burnham  well I think everybody has to make their  own decisions based on what they feel it  is best i think there's honor in leaving  and there's honor in staying and I think  you know trying to divide the party into  those two camps it's not a helpful thing  to do I know they're of colleagues might  have liked me to stay and I know there  are colleagues of mine who wished I  hadn't accepted a job in the first place  but I do what I think is best for my  area for myself as a Labour MP  representing those views and I'll always  put that first regard so what are the  colleagues may think about that so I'm  happy for everyone to make their own  decision on this and I don't I don't  object to anyone staying or indeed  leaving if they've done that after that  after I've made my decision today mr.  Reynolds will you sleep safer in your  bed knowing that Emily Thornberry is in  charge of your policies views on defense  well Jeremy is clearly one today a one  view to be coming from the senior  members of the the shadow cabinet on  defence policy clearly that is awkward  for some of us because it's different to  what official party policy is and young  we're going to resolve that it's about  having perhaps a proper process at  conference a decision that we decide  we're going to continue with our  existing policy or move to something  different but clearly I've got  objections to some of the things that  now becoming officially from a front  bench on that and again the only  honorable thing to do to respond to that  is to make sure you're able to speak  freely about what you think is right for  your area because of that but yeah Kevin  Jones who knows a lot about defense says  that she knows nothing about defense he  also said that the Deputy Leader Tom  Watson had serious reservations about  her being offered the job nevertheless  she was what do you think of that well  those are strong words I think you know  a lot of Labour MPs are comfortable with  the official policy of the leg party on  defense matters and that will be the  case at all levels of the party but  clearly there are different views on  this they've always been different views  stretching back really back to the 1950s  when this first became an issue when the  first labour government would come  Natalie took that decision to get us a  nuclear weapon in the UK and for us to  be a nuclear power there's always been a  split on this so it comes up time and  time again but clearly it's very  difficult but I understand why Jeremy  wants a defence secretary who has the  same view as him but obviously I'd like  him to try and understand where other  members with different views to him  coming from within the PLP to final  thought just before we let you go mr.  Reynolds looking at the lineup of the  shadow cabinet and the shadow ministers  as they stand today are they a good team  to perhaps lead the country come the  next election well it could be I think  nearly every member laid part will  probably regret the last few days have  been spent talking about a reshuffle  rather than about the flooding problems  rather than about the rail fare  increases and of course the  Conservatives problems that they've got  with the EU referendum so if we are able  to take on the Tories that would be a  good team doing that but I think what I  am worried about is we're giving the  wrong impression with some of the  decisions that we've made in this region  particularly on foreign policy and we  won't get a chance frankly to make that  argument for a fairer better Britain if  people are worried about our credentials  on defense and foreign policy great to  talk to you thanks for joining us on Sky  News

